# Line endings are LF everywhere, in the repository and in the working tree.
#
# Without this the site was published from three places that disagreed: a Windows checkout writing
# CRLF, the GitHub Actions runner writing LF, and a browser upload route that sends raw bytes to the
# blob API and never passes through git's filters at all. The first nightly run produced a commit of
# 2,725 insertions and 2,725 deletions in which nothing had actually changed.
#
# eol=lf rather than the default native checkout, so a Windows working tree matches what the runner
# and the upload route produce. There are no .bat, .cmd or .ps1 files here. The one exception is
# calendar files, handled below.
#
# This alone is not enough, which is why _tools/sync.js also normalises line endings before it
# fingerprints a page or hashes an asset. A blob uploaded through the API can still arrive with CRLF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Never touched: these are binary and any substitution would corrupt them.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.webp binary
*.ico  binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.pdf  binary
*.mp4  binary
*.webm binary

# Calendar files are the one place CRLF is correct: RFC 5545 specifies it as the line ending, and
# some calendar clients are strict about it. -text means git stores and checks out the bytes exactly
# as written, on every platform, so these stay CRLF while the rest of the site stays LF.
*.ics  -text
